<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">St Andrews</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saint Andrews, Cennrigmonaid, Kinrimund</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">St Andrews is a town on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fife">Fife</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, known earlier as Cennrigmonaid. A Pictish church there received relics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-the-apostle">Saint Andrew</a> by tradition in the 8th century, and it became the seat of Scotland's chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, raised to an archbishopric in 1472. Its great cathedral now lies in ruins. The University of St Andrews, founded in 1413, is Scotland's oldest, and the town is known as the home of golf.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/st-andrews.jpg" alt="St Andrews" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Gordon · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Andrews_from_Regulus_tower_-_geograph.org.uk_-_254003.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
